-- title:
--   Primitivity criterion: additive coboundary modulo the defining ideal
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be a field, let $f\colon A\to\operatorname{Spec}K$ be a scheme over $K$ and let $L$ be a relative group law on $f$, i.e. a functorial group structure on the sets $\mathrm{SchemeHomOver}(t,f)$ of sections of $f$ over a base morphism $t$, with multiplication, unit and inverse compatible with base change. Fix $n\in\mathbb N$, a two-dimensional formal group law $F$ over $K$ (two power series in $\mathrm{Fin}\,2\oplus\mathrm{Fin}\,2$ variables with vanishing constant term, the expected linear coefficients and the associativity identity) and a system $\theta$ of formal coordinates, assigning to each $K$-algebra $B'$ and each pair $s\in (B')^2$ a point $\theta_{B'}(s)$ over $\operatorname{Spec}B'$, assumed to satisfy $L.\mathrm{IsFormalCoordinates}\,F\,\theta$: $\theta$ commutes with base change along $K$-algebra maps on nilpotent tuples, and for every ideal $J$ with $J^{m+1}=0$ it carries tuples in $J$ injectively onto the points that become the unit modulo $J$, and converts the truncated evaluation $F.\mathrm{nilMul}_m$ into $L.\mathrm{mul}$. Let $\varphi\colon \mathrm{Fin}\,2\to K[\![X_1,X_2]\!]$, let $H$ be a commutative Hopf $K$-algebra, and let $e$ be a family of bijections, for each $K$-algebra $T$, from $\mathrm{Hom}_{K\text{-alg}}(H,T)$ with its convolution monoid structure onto the set of $x\in\mathrm{SchemeHomOver}(\operatorname{Spec}T\to\operatorname{Spec}K,f)$ with $n\cdot x$ the unit, such that $e_T(\chi\ast\psi)=L.\mathrm{mul}(e_T(\chi),e_T(\psi))$. Let $\nu\in\mathbb N$ and let $\Phi\colon K[\![X_1,X_2]\!]/(\varphi_1,\varphi_2)\xrightarrow{\ \sim\ }H$ be a $K$-algebra isomorphism, pinned by the hypothesis that for every $K$-algebra $T$ and every $\chi\colon H\to T$, writing $c_\chi=(\chi\Phi\bar X_i)_i$: there is an ideal $J\subseteq T$ with $J^{\nu+1}=0$ containing all $c_\chi(i)$; $\chi(\Phi\bar G)=\mathrm{nilEval}_\nu(G)(c_\chi)$ for every power series $G$, where $\mathrm{nilEval}_\nu$ evaluates the truncation of $G$ in degrees $\le\nu$ in each variable; and $e_T(\chi)=\theta_T(c_\chi)$. Assume further that every nilpotent zero of $\varphi$ is realised: for every $K$-algebra $T$, ideal $J$ with $J^{m+1}=0$ and every $s\in J^2$ with $\mathrm{nilEval}_m(\varphi_i)(s)=0$ for both $i$, there is $\chi$ with $c_\chi=s$. Then for every $G\in K[\![X_1,X_2]\!]$, the element $\Phi(\bar G)$ lies in $\mathrm{primitives}\,K\,H$, the kernel of $x\mapsto \Delta x-x\otimes 1-1\otimes x$, if and only if the constant coefficient of $G$ vanishes and the additive coboundary $F.\mathrm{addCoboundary}\,G=G(F(X,Y))-G(X)-G(Y)$ lies in the ideal of $K[\![X_1,X_2,Y_1,Y_2]\!]$ generated by the $\varphi_l$ substituted in the first block of variables together with the $\varphi_l$ substituted in the second block.
--
--   This is the translation of primitivity in the Hopf algebra $H$ of the $n$-torsion of the relative group law into the formal-group $2$-cocycle condition on a representing power series, along a pinned presentation $H\cong K[\![X_1,X_2]\!]/(\varphi_1,\varphi_2)$ compatible with the formal coordinates $\theta$. It is used in the construction, for ramified places in the Čerednik–Drinfeld setting, of a special formal $\mathcal O_D$-module structure together with a linear map on primitives.
